1. What is throw?

throw is a keyword used to explicitly throw an exception.

Syntax:

throw new ExceptionType("Message");

Example:

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        throw new ArithmeticException("Number cannot be zero");
    }
}

Output

Exception in thread "main"
java.lang.ArithmeticException: Number cannot be zero
2. What is throws?

throws is used in the method declaration to tell the caller that this method may throw one or more exceptions.

Example

import java.io.*;

class Demo {

    static void readFile() throws IOException {

        FileReader fr = new FileReader("abc.txt");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

    }
}
3. Difference between throw and throws
throw	throws
Keyword	Keyword
Used inside method	Used in method declaration
Throws one exception object	Declares one or more exception types
Requires new keyword	Doesn't require new
Transfers control immediately	Only informs the caller

Example

throw new IOException();
void display() throws IOException {

}
4. Can we throw Checked Exception?

Yes.

import java.io.IOException;

class Demo {

    static void display() throws IOException {

        throw new IOException("File Missing");
    }
}
5. Can we throw Unchecked Exception?

Yes.

throw new NullPointerException();
6. Can we throw Error?

Yes.

Although generally not recommended.

throw new StackOverflowError();
7. Can we throw Throwable?

Yes.

throw new Throwable();

But almost never done in real projects.

8. Can we throw Object?

No.

throw new Object();

Compile-time Error

Reason:

Only subclasses of Throwable can be thrown.

Hierarchy

Object
   │
Throwable
 ├── Exception
 └── Error
9. Can we throw String?

No.

throw "Error";

Compile-time Error.

Unlike Python or JavaScript.

10. Can we throw null?

Yes.

throw null;

Output

Exception in thread "main"
java.lang.NullPointerException

The JVM tries to throw a Throwable, but the reference is null.

11. Can one method throw multiple exceptions?

Yes.

void display() throws IOException, SQLException {

}
12. Can throw throw multiple exceptions at once?

No.

Wrong

throw new IOException(), new SQLException();

One throw statement can throw only one exception object.

13. Can we write multiple throws?

No.

Wrong

void display() throws IOException throws SQLException {

}

Correct

void display() throws IOException, SQLException {

}
14. Can constructor use throws?

Yes.

class Demo {

    Demo() throws IOException {

    }
}
15. Can constructor throw exception?

Yes.

class Demo {

    Demo() {

        throw new RuntimeException();
    }
}
16. Can static method use throws?

Yes.

static void display() throws IOException {

}
17. Can main method use throws?

Yes.

public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

}
18. Why does main method often declare throws Exception?

Instead of handling every checked exception, it lets the JVM receive any uncaught exception and terminate the program with a stack trace. This is common in small demos but less common in production applications.

19. Can we throw checked exception without throws?

No.

void display() {

    throw new IOException();
}

Compile-time Error

Reason:

Checked exceptions must either be:

caught
or declared using throws
20. Can unchecked exception be thrown without throws?

Yes.

void display() {

    throw new ArithmeticException();
}

Perfectly valid.

21. Is throws mandatory?

Only for checked exceptions.

22. Is throw mandatory?

No.

It is used only when you want to explicitly raise an exception.

23. Can throws be used with RuntimeException?

Yes.

void display() throws ArithmeticException {

}

Valid, but unnecessary because runtime exceptions don't need to be declared.

24. Can overriding method throw broader checked exception?

No.

class Parent {

    void display() throws IOException {

    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void display() throws Exception {

    }
}

Compile-time Error.

Reason:

A child cannot widen the checked exceptions declared by the parent method.

25. Can child throw smaller checked exception?

Yes.

class Parent {

    void display() throws Exception {

    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void display() throws IOException {

    }
}

Valid.

26. Can child throw RuntimeException?

Yes.

class Parent {

    void display() {

    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void display() throws ArithmeticException {

    }
}

Valid.

Runtime exceptions are unrestricted in overriding.

27. Can overriding method remove throws?

Yes.

class Parent {

    void display() throws IOException {

    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void display() {

    }
}

Valid.

The child can declare fewer or no checked exceptions.

28. Can interface method use throws?

Yes.

interface Demo {

    void display() throws IOException;
}
29. Can implementing class remove throws?

Yes.

interface Demo {

    void display() throws IOException;
}

class Test implements Demo {

    public void display() {

    }
}

Valid.

30. Can implementing class throw broader checked exception?

No.

interface Demo {

    void display() throws IOException;
}

class Test implements Demo {

    public void display() throws Exception {

    }
}

Compile-time Error.

31. Can abstract method use throws?

Yes.

abstract void display() throws IOException;
32. Can lambda expression throw checked exception?

Only if the functional interface method declares it.

Example:

@FunctionalInterface
interface Reader {
    void read() throws IOException;
}

Reader r = () -> {
    throw new IOException("Read failed");
};
33. Can we throw custom exception?

Yes.

class InvalidAgeException extends Exception {

    InvalidAgeException(String msg) {
        super(msg);
    }
}

Use

throw new InvalidAgeException("Age must be 18+");
34. Why do we create custom exceptions?

To represent business-specific errors instead of generic exceptions.

Examples:

InvalidAgeException
InsufficientBalanceException
UserNotFoundException
ProductOutOfStockException
35. What happens after throw?

Execution stops at that point in the current method.

Example

System.out.println("A");

throw new RuntimeException();

System.out.println("B");

Output

A

B is never printed.

36. Can we write code after throw?
throw new RuntimeException();

System.out.println("Hello");

Compile-time Error

Unreachable statement
37. Can throw be inside catch?

Yes.

try {

}
catch(Exception e){

    throw e;
}

This is called rethrowing the exception.

38. Can throw be inside finally?

Yes.

finally {

    throw new RuntimeException();
}

Be careful: if finally throws an exception, it replaces any exception already being propagated.

39. Can throws be used with final method?

Yes.

final void display() throws IOException {

}

final and throws are unrelated.

40. Can synchronized method use throws?

Yes.

synchronized void display() throws IOException {

}
41. Can private method use throws?

Yes.

private void display() throws IOException {

}
42. Can recursive method use throws?

Yes.

void fun() throws IOException {

    fun();
}
43. Can generic method use throws?

Yes.

<T> void display() throws IOException {

}
44. What is exception propagation?

If a method doesn't handle an exception, it passes it to its caller.

Example:

void c() throws IOException {
    throw new IOException();
}

void b() throws IOException {
    c();
}

void a() throws IOException {
    b();
}

The exception propagates from c() → b() → a() → caller.

45. Real-world Spring Boot example
public UserDto getUser(int id) {

    User user = userRepository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException(
                    "User not found with id " + id));

    return modelMapper.map(user, UserDto.class);
}

Here:

orElseThrow() uses throw internally to create and throw an exception.
throws is not required because ResourceNotFoundException usually extends RuntimeException (an unchecked exception).